    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#alexander-nevsky"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky.jpg" alt="Alexander Nevsky" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlexanderNevskyTitul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Collective of Kremlin Armory artists, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a>, a small fortified settlement on the Moskva River first mentioned in the chronicles under 1147, lay on the western edge of the Rus' principality of Vladimir-Suzdal, which had submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a> after their invasion of 1237–1240. On the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> in 1263 the town passed, according to tradition, to his youngest son Daniel, then a small child, and under him it became the seat of a separate line of princes. Daniel enlarged his modest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#appanage">appanage</a> by taking Kolomna from the Principality of Ryazan around 1301 and inheriting Pereslavl-Zalessky in 1302. His son Yury married a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uzbek-khan">Uzbek Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and obtained the khan's patent (<em>yarlyk</em>) for the grand principality of Vladimir in 1317, beginning a bitter rivalry with the princes of Tver. When Tver rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tatars">Tatars</a> in 1327, Yury's brother Ivan I Kalita joined the Horde's punitive expedition and was rewarded with the grand princely title, which he held alone from 1331. Metropolitan Peter, head of the Rus' church, settled in Moscow around 1325 and died there in 1326, and his successor Theognostus made the town his residence, giving the young dynasty a religious centre to add to the lucrative right, which came with the grand princely title, to collect the Horde's tribute from the other Rus' princes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#moscow"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="Moscow" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_and_the_Red_Square.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Department of State, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muscovite society was overwhelmingly rural. Peasants grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rye">rye</a>, oats and barley on the poor soils of the forest zone, supplementing farming with fishing, hunting, beekeeping and the trapping of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fur-trade">furs</a>. Most peasants were still free to move, although the law code of 1497 restricted their right to leave a landlord to the weeks around St George's Day in late November. Above them stood the grand prince, his kin and a limited circle of boyar families, served by lesser servitors, while monasteries became great landowners: the Trinity Monastery founded by Sergius of Radonezh around the 1340s inspired disciples who founded new monasteries across the northern forests and helped to colonise them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a> itself grew into a large town of wooden houses around the Kremlin, home to craftsmen, icon painters and merchants. Furs, wax, honey and leather were the main exports, traded through Novgorod to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#volga">Volga</a> to Kazan and the steppe, and south towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crimea">Crimea</a>, where Moscow's merchants known as <em>surozhane</em> dealt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silks</a> and other luxuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> shaped daily life and culture, and the icons of Andrei Rublev, above all his <em>Trinity</em> of the early fifteenth century, mark the high point of Muscovite religious art.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#dmitry-donskoy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy.jpg" alt="Dmitry Donskoy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dmitri_Donskoy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown. Died over 100 years ago, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muscovy's armies were built around the cavalry of the grand prince's court and the retinues of boyars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#appanage">appanage</a> princes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> who fought much like their steppe opponents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> replaced the oak walls of the Kremlin with white limestone in 1367–1368, and the new fortress withstood sieges by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algirdas">Algirdas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1368 and 1370. On 8 September 1380 Dmitry led a coalition of Rus' princes to victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tatars">Tatar</a> commander Mamai at the Battle of Kulikovo on the upper Don, but two years later Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> took and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a>, and in 1408 the emir Edigu besieged it again. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> the army grew larger and better organised. His forces crushed Novgorod at the Battle of the Shelon River in 1471, and in 1480 he faced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#akhmat-khan">Akhmat Khan</a> of the Great Horde across the Ugra River until the Tatars withdrew without a battle, an episode remembered as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-stand-on-the-ugra-river">Great Stand on the Ugra River</a>. Wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> in 1487–1494 and 1500–1503, including the victory at the Battle of Vedrosha in 1500, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chernigov">Chernigov</a> and Seversk lands. Vasily III captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#smolensk">Smolensk</a> in 1514, although a Polish-Lithuanian army defeated his forces at the Battle of Orsha the same year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#field-artillery">Cannon</a> and Italian-designed brick fortifications appeared from the 1470s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ulugh-muhammad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulugh-muhammad.jpg" alt="Ulugh Muhammad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ulugh-muhammad">Ulugh Muhammad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_reconstruction_(2023-03-07)_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vyacheslav Kirillin, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muscovite government grew out of the grand prince's household. Land and authority were divided by testament among the ruler's sons as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#appanage">appanages</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a>'s princes increasingly favoured the eldest son, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a>'s will of 1389 treated the grand principality of Vladimir as hereditary property for the first time. The principle was tested in the civil war of 1425–1453, when Vasily II fought his uncle Yury of Zvenigorod and his cousins Vasily Kosoy and Dmitry Shemyaka; Vasily was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tatars">Tatars</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ulugh-muhammad">Ulugh Muhammad</a> in 1445 and blinded by his rivals in 1446, yet regained the throne with the backing of the church and the boyars, and his victory ended claims by collateral lines. A council of boyars advised the ruler, governors (<em>namestniki</em>) ran districts and lived off fees known as <em>kormlenie</em>, or 'feeding', and from the late fifteenth century state secretaries (<em>dyaki</em>) kept written records. After the annexation of Novgorod, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> confiscated estates and granted them as <em>pomestie</em>, land held in return for military service, creating a cavalry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gentry">gentry</a> dependent on the crown. His law code, the Sudebnik of 1497, standardised court procedure. Ivan's marriage to Sophia Palaiologina in 1472, his use of the double-headed eagle and the title 'sovereign of all Rus'' expressed a growing claim to autocratic, quasi-imperial authority.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#nizhny-novgorod"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod.jpg" alt="Nizhny Novgorod" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nizhny_Novgorod_2025-04-29_Minin_and_Pozharsky_square_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AlexTref871, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muscovy turned a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#appanage">appanage</a> into the largest state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> and fixed the political and religious pattern of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a> for centuries. Its princes 'gathered the Russian lands' by purchase, inheritance, marriage, diplomacy and war, absorbing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> in 1392, Yaroslavl in 1463, Rostov in 1474, the Novgorod Republic in 1478, Tver in 1485, Vyatka in 1489, Pskov in 1510 and Ryazan around 1521. The fall of the city republics ended the alternative tradition of assemblies (<em>veche</em>) that Novgorod and Pskov had represented. In church affairs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a> rejected the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> agreed at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#florence">Florence</a> in 1439, and in 1448 Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishops</a> chose Jonah as metropolitan without approval from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a>, a de facto independence confirmed after the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fall-of-constantinople">conquest of Constantinople</a> in 1453. Writers such as the monk Philotheus of Pskov later presented Moscow as the 'Third Rome', heir of a fallen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, an idea that shaped Russian self-understanding. Italian architects, among them Aristotele Fioravanti, who built the Cathedral of the Dormition in 1475–1479, gave the Moscow Kremlin much of its present form. Muscovy also absorbed steppe practices in taxation, postal relays and diplomacy, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tatars">Tatar</a> princes such as those of the Qasim Khanate into its service and set the pattern for later expansion into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#volga">Volga</a> basin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siberia">Siberia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ivan-iv-of-russia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia.jpg" alt="Ivan IV of Russia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV of Russia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IoannIV_reconstruction_by_Gerasimov02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shakko, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duchy did not collapse but was transformed. Vasily III (r. 1505–1533) completed his father's work, ending the independence of Pskov in 1510, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#smolensk">Smolensk</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1514 and absorbing the last Ryazan principality around 1521, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a> repeatedly intervened in the succession of the Khanate of Kazan. By his death the state stretched from the White Sea to the steppe frontier south of the Oka and from Smolensk towards the Urals. His heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> was three years old, and the regency of his mother Elena Glinskaya (1533–1538) was followed by years of rivalry between boyar clans such as the Shuiskys and Belskys, which nonetheless left the institutions of the state intact. On 16 January 1547 Metropolitan Macarius crowned the sixteen-year-old Ivan in the Cathedral of the Dormition as tsar, a title derived from Caesar that earlier rulers had used only occasionally. The coronation marked the end of the grand duchy as a political form and the beginning of the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a>, which conquered Kazan in 1552 and Astrakhan in 1556, became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> in 1721 and passed on Moscow's autocratic traditions to the modern Russian state. Moscow lost its status as capital to St Petersburg in the early eighteenth century and regained it in 1918.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Grand Duchy of Moscow turned a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#appanage">appanage</a> into the core of the Russian state, using service to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> and two centuries of annexations to end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tatars">Tatar</a> overlordship in 1480 and lay the foundations of Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#autocracy">autocracy</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> was crowned tsar in 1547.</em></p>

